{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310867,"nonce":"MNQtFmtYjPvu/Sb+wZNIG5/Li58=","title":"TensorPool defaults OFF (RAYZOR_POOL=1 opt-in); pool=DISABLED indicator + real PoolStats now surface in --alloc-stats dump (commit ...","message":"[[bugs-decode-loop-per-step-leak]] noted \"pool_hit_rate=0.0% pool_parked=0 pool_evicted=0\" in the `[tensor-data]` dump from `rayzor debug run --alloc-stats`. The natural read was \"pool is broken — alloc churn is leaking through it.\" A workflow investigation found two distinct problems:\n\n1. **The pool is opt-in (`RAYZOR_POOL=1`)** — defaults to disabled state (`tensor_pool.rs:772`). `rayzor debug run` does NOT export `RAYZOR_POOL=1`, so every `--alloc-stats` run before the fix was profiling the disabled path.\n2. **`TENSOR_POOL_PARKED` and `TENSOR_POOL_EVICTED` were vestigial counters** — declared at `tensor.rs:43-44` as `AtomicU64::new(0)` but never `fetch_add`'d anywhere. The real pool counters live on `PoolStats { pushes, evictions, current_bytes, peak_bytes }` in `tensor_pool.rs:307-313`, and were only printed by the SEPARATE atexit hook gated by `RAYZOR_POOL_STATS=1`.\n\nSo the dump output was unfalsifiable: pool=enabled with great hit rate and pool=disabled with zero hit rate produced identical-looking lines.\n\n**Why:** the pool is intentionally opt-in (memory-vs-throughput tradeoff for long-running processes); the vestigial counters appear to be leftover from an earlier dual-counter design.\n\n**How to apply:** future leak investigations on `[tensor-data]` lines should check `pool=enabled` or `pool=DISABLED` first. If `pool=DISABLED` and you want pool stats, re-run with `RAYZOR_POOL=1`.\n\n\nPost-fix `[tensor-data]` line shape (Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M, 64-tok decode, M1 Pro):\n\n`pool=DISABLED`:\n- `pool_hits=0 pool_misses=17178 pool_hit_rate=0.0% pool_pushes=11252 pool_evictions=11252 pool_current_bytes=0`\n- Explicit `pool=DISABLED` prefix tells the reader the pool wasn't tried.\n\n`pool=enabled` (RAYZOR_POOL=1):\n- `pool_hits=11233 pool_misses=5945 pool_hit_rate=65.4% pool_pushes=11252 pool_evictions=0 pool_current_bytes=13792256 pool_peak_bytes=13792256`\n- 65.4% reuse rate confirmed; ~14 MB parked at steady-state.\n\nChanges:\n- New `TensorPool::is_disabled()` accessor at `tensor_pool.rs:373-376`.\n- `rayzor_dump_tensor_alloc_stats` reads from `tensor_pool::global().stats.snapshot()` instead of vestigial atomics.\n- KV file gains `pool_disabled`, `pool_pushes`, `pool_evictions`, `pool_current_bytes`, `pool_peak_bytes`. Old `pool_parked` / `pool_evicted` keys removed (the `rayzor debug server` KV consumer only reads `pool_hits` / `pool_misses` per `src/debug/server.rs:157-172`, no break).\n\n\nPaired A/B `RAYZOR_POOL=1` vs default on the same 64-token Paris-history workload, alternating ON/OFF, N=15 pairs:\n\n- ON median  = 20.984 tok/s\n- OFF median = 20.921 tok/s\n- **+0.30% median win**, sign test 10/15, p=0.30 — sub-noise\n- Robust (drop |delta|≥2.0 outliers): +0.067 tok/s, 8/13 sign\n\nThe pool reuses ~65% of tensor allocs but mutex acquisition + bucket walk balance the saved malloc cost. **No reason to flip the default ON** based on M1 Pro decode-wall measurement. Memory tradeoff (peak +13 MB parked) is acceptable on long generations.\n\n\nWithout this fix every future `--alloc-stats` investigation would silently see `pool=DISABLED` and misread it as a broken-but-active pool. Same trap I fell into this session. Future leak investigations get one fewer source of fake leads.\n\n\n- [[bugs-decode-loop-per-step-leak]] — the previous bug memory; \"0% hit rate\" residual was the lead that motivated this investigation. Outcome: pool was just disabled.\n- [[bugs-q4km-sdot-tile-no-win]] — the alternating-pair A/B discipline used here is the same one that calibrated the Q4_K_M tile measurement.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_tensor_pool_observability` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}